# std/merge's image (design/caos-expr.md, Phase 3): this directory is a flake
# (flake.nix + flake.lock + worker), so its image is the flake-builder applied
# to the directory itself. It names the builder by a LOCAL deep-deps mount
# (DEEP-DEPS/flake-builder, declared in DEPS) -- the only way it CAN name it,
# since nothing resolves a builtin by name. Resolving this entry evaluates the
# expression, which makes the flake->image step explicit rather than leaving it
# to the server to detect.
#
# The `help` is bound HERE so that every harness offering this tool describes it
# the same way. It used to be a string literal inside llm-step, which meant a
# second harness had to copy it -- and a copied description drifts.
#
# `@param theirs` is the only thing a caller supplies. `ours` is the workspace
# it is merging into, which the launch binds; a model naming it would be naming
# the conversation it is already in.
#
# `@writer` is what tells the harness this tool's result is a PROPOSAL rather
# than a value (SPEC, "CaosTools"). It used to be known by matching the tool's
# NAME in llm-step, which is why no other tool could ever propose one.
#
# THIS TEXT IS NOW THE ONLY COPY. llm-step carried a second, hand-written
# declaration of this tool and used that one instead of this -- the exact drift
# the note above warns about, and the two had already diverged on whether they
# called it a workspace or a source tree.
HELP=<<END
Three-way merge another commit into a source tree. `theirs` is the commit to merge in: a full commit hash already imported into CAOS, or the path of a source tree in the conversation, such as one `import-source` made (imports/repo/main-2). A branch NAME is not accepted: import the branch first, which observes the remote and gives you the tree to merge. The current side is `source-tree` as it is now. A clean merge advances the source tree to the merged result. A conflict advances it too, with git's inline conflict markers in the files and a reserved `.caos/conflicts` file listing every unresolved path -- including structural conflicts (delete/modify, mode, binary) that have NO markers. Resolve each: edit the file (use `read` with `in` set to the stage's tree to inspect its content), then delete that path's rows from `.caos/conflicts`. Saving the resolution removes the empty ledger and its .caos directory if empty. Bash changes are committed automatically, just like inline edits. Then run relevant checks.
@param source-tree Path of the source tree to merge into (a code reference in the conversation), such as feature/01-change.
@param {commit} theirs A full commit hash, or the path of a source tree in the conversation.
@param {commit} [merge-base] Full commit hash to use as the merge base instead of the one git finds. Only for replaying a change onto a history that does not contain its old base, such as a stack layer rebuilt on a force-pushed upstream: merge-base is then the old tip of the layer below.
@writer
@scope source-tree
END
IMAGE=run --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/flake-builder --in:@=. --lock:@=DEEP-DEPS/flake.lock
curry --base=$IMAGE --help=$HELP
